# Never commit or push directly to main

`main` is the release branch. `release-please` parses every commit that lands on
it to decide the next version and write `CHANGELOG.md`, and CI only gates
`pull_request` events. Always work on a branch and open a PR.

```bash
# BAD: unreviewed, CI-ungated commits land straight on the release branch
git commit -m "feat: add cached run lookup" && git push origin main

# GOOD
git switch -c feat/cached-run-lookup
git commit -m "feat: add cached run lookup"
git push -u origin HEAD
gh pr create --title "feat: add cached run lookup" --body "..."
```

Never force-push `main`, and never hand-edit `version` in `pyproject.toml` or
`.release-please-manifest.json` — release-please owns both.

## Why this matters for releases

- Squash-merge so the PR title becomes the single conventional commit on `main`.
  Pushing a branch's raw commits means each one is parsed, which clutters
  `CHANGELOG.md` and can bump the version differently than intended.
- The PR title must follow Conventional Commits, since that is what
  release-please reads. See `CONTRIBUTING.md` for types and their bump effects;
  use `!` or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer for breaking changes.
- CI runs `pre-commit run --all-files` and `pytest` on `pull_request`. Pushing
  to `main` skips that gate, so a broken commit becomes immediately releasable.

## Releasing

release-please opens a Release PR on `main` once releasable commits land.
Merging that PR tags the GitHub release and publishes to PyPI. That PR is the
only thing that should change the version or `CHANGELOG.md`.
